Refuse to shadow a resource whose memory belongs to somebody else.

fd_try_shadow_resource swaps the backing bo's (freedreno_resource.c:431) and
releases the old one once the shadow's batches retire (:442-446). A resource
imported through fd_resource_from_handle has a client holding a mapping of that
memory for the resource's whole life: the AIR shim maps once at creation
(air_metal.c:464-474, "Mapped once, for the buffer's whole life") and never maps
again, so after a swap the client writes into a bo this driver intends to free.

fd_resource_from_handle sets is_shared on exactly those resources (:1527), and
no code in freedreno reads the flag: `grep -rn is_shared` over the driver
directory finds four assignments (:1097, :1391, :1527, :1742) and no condition.
The one shadow-related guard, :1116, tests the PIPE_BIND_SHARED bind bit, which
air_metal.c:1186-1188 deliberately leaves out of the import template.

Refusing here is a path the driver already handles. :1116 shadows only when
!(prsc->bind & PIPE_BIND_SHARED), so a resource carrying that bind bit already
reaches a failed fd_screen_bo_get_handle with no shadow. The other two callers
fall back: :906 takes the staging branch at :910, which :892-893 already calls
the better choice when shadowing would not avoid a flush, and :554
(fd_resource_uncompress) asserts on failure, so a debug build stops and a release
build returns without decompressing. That site is reached only for a
UBWC-compressed resource (a6xx/fd6_image.cc:443, a6xx/fd6_resource.cc:218,225),
and an imported resource carries a linear modifier.

Measured facts this change rests on. Two rounds (E with fb_hz=120, F with the
default 60) produced the same fault at the same call site: 28 of 28 link
registers satisfy (lr - 0x27000c) mod 0x4000 == 0, which is the return address of
the bl to _memcpy at libredwoodair.dylib + 0x270008, inside _util_copy_rect, the
row-loop memcpy at u_format.c:94. pc minus the cache slide is 0x2a53619c0 in both
rounds. The destination is memory a client is using: each faulting x0 sits inside
a burst of same-pid "rwa buffer: copied ... buffer at <x0>" lines, 2 to 8 lines
before the fault and 12 to 402 after it, so it is that buffer's _contents, and
four of the addresses recur across boots. The source argument holds bytes of
freed memory reused by the process's own allocator: ObjC selector fragments
("le.siri.", "le.board", "le.Atten", "ion: _ZN", "cklight-") and UUID fragments
("6-B233-4", "2-2CA8-4"). Copy lengths are 192 (13 times in E, 15 in F), 355
(once in E) and 38 (once in F); each is one row, ceil(width / blockwidth) *
blocksize, so 355 pins a single-channel 8-bit format while 192 and 38 do not.
The row is the mapped box's, not the resource's: u_surface.c:231 takes the box
from the caller, and both AIR read-backs pass the region the client asked for
(air_metal.c:1652 and :1745, from redwoodmetal_texture.inc:140-142 and
:165-168), so the resource can be much wider than any of the three lengths.
None of the three divides 32768, the buffer size those copies run against, so
bytesPerRow and the row length are not the same quantity and the buffer size only
bounds rows * stride. Every faulting x0 is 16K aligned while the loop advances
dst by dst_stride, which reads as a fault on the first row: the source pointer
was already invalid when the map returned it, and u_surface.c:330 only rejects
NULL.

Alternatives kept for the next step, in order. Adding PIPE_BIND_SHARED to the
import template (and repeating the is_format_supported query with that bit, which
is why air_metal.c:1186-1188 left it out) fixes the wrong assumption itself but
covers only the :1116 caller. Setting prsc->bind after the import covers the same
one caller and depends on :1116's implementation. Replacing the map-once-for-life
buffer model in air_metal.c:464-474 covers both the bo swap and a staging
substitution and costs the most.

Why the round that verifies this has to look at the picture and not only at the
fault count. The caller at :554 is fd_resource_uncompress, whose :556-557 is
`ASSERTED bool success = ...; assert(success);`. A release build compiles the
assert out, so this guard would make the function return without decompressing
and its caller would read compressed data as linear: wrong pixels rather than a
crash, and nothing in the log. Its three callers are in the a6xx backend
(a6xx/fd6_image.cc:443, a6xx/fd6_resource.cc:218,225) and are reached for a
UBWC-compressed resource, while an imported one carries a linear modifier, so
this is not expected to fire. Expected is not measured, which is why accepting
this round takes three readings: bad accesses at zero, the desktop still drawn
correctly, and a frame count comparable with a healthy round.

Verification. mk-mesa.sh:106 stamps PATCHES with the sha256 of cat
tools/mesa/*.patch, so a new patch file re-extracts and rebuilds the tree
(:107-118); no list needs updating, and no other patch touches this file. :115
applies them with `|| [ "$REBASE" = 26 ]`, so under REBASE=26 a patch that fails
to apply does not stop the build. Confirm the guard is in the built tree with
`grep -c 'if (rsc->b.is_shared)' <tree>/src/gallium/drivers/freedreno/freedreno_resource.c`,
0 before and 1 after. Counting every occurrence of the expression instead gives 4
before and 5 after, because the assignments at :1097, :1391, :1527 and :1742
already use it. Also confirm the rebuilt libredwoodair.dylib sha256 differs from
the frozen one. Check this file with `git apply --check` rather than BSD patch:
macOS patch 2.0-12u11-Apple reports it malformed at the first added line because
of the prose above, while the container's GNU patch, which is what mk-mesa.sh:115
runs, applies it.

diff --git a/src/gallium/drivers/freedreno/freedreno_resource.c b/src/gallium/drivers/freedreno/freedreno_resource.c
--- a/src/gallium/drivers/freedreno/freedreno_resource.c
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/freedreno/freedreno_resource.c
@@ -353,8 +353,26 @@
    struct pipe_resource *prsc = &rsc->b.b;
    struct fd_screen *screen = fd_screen(pctx->screen);
    struct fd_batch *batch;
    bool fallback = false;
 
+   /* Shadowing swaps the backing bo's (:431) and releases the old one once the
+    * shadow's batches retire (:442-446). For a resource imported from memory
+    * somebody else allocated, a client holds a mapping of that memory for the
+    * resource's whole life and does not map again, so after a swap it writes
+    * into a bo this driver intends to free. fd_resource_from_handle marks those
+    * resources is_shared (:1527); nothing else in this driver reads the flag.
+    *
+    * The caller at :1116 already leaves a PIPE_BIND_SHARED resource unshadowed
+    * when fd_screen_bo_get_handle fails, :906 falls back to the staging branch
+    * at :910, and :554 asserts. Refusing is the handled path in all three.
+    *
+    * Returning here also skips the flushes this function does at :365 and
+    * :376-378. Those are for the modification shadowing is about to make; the
+    * resource is left untouched, and each caller's fallback does its own.
+    */
+   if (rsc->b.is_shared)
+      return false;
+
    if (prsc->next)
       return false;
 
